Thessians are a cadre of beings from beyond the [[Phenomena/Firmament|firmament]], the apex of life within the [[Geography/index|Astra]] and antithetical to mortal life. They live by drawing energy out of the stars and planets of a crystal sphere, which kills those who live there. They seek to reverse entropy, and reversing it will end all life in the universe. The one true threat they see is the heat death of the Astra, when there will be no crystal spheres left to consume.

The Thessians began on Old Thessia, a world near the core that drifted too close to it. Its Higher Kind, [[Divinity/Formless|Formless]], gave up its name and form to grant its divinity to a scant few of its people, and those few became the Thessians. A hundred underwent Ascension from Formless' gift, and these are the Ascended Thessians. The powerful are said to bar anyone from returning to Old Thessia, and the city the [[Divinity/Unknown Entities/The Deep|Deep]] raised on [[Geography/Althane/Realms/Ilkyne|Ilkyne]] is held to be a copy of one from it. The Deep they called the biggest of them, until [[Org/Althane/The Weft|the Weft]] destroyed it at the top of the City of Yellow. When the Weft were shown the origin of the Thessians, it was the three-point starfish that turned a body gray and tri-radial, and [[People/Althane/Lev|Lev]] took that for their descent.

They were not born gods. They ascend in tiers, each generation further from what it was, and each narrows to a single subject that becomes its whole nature: the one [[Org/Sedecium|Sedecium]] calls [[Divinity/Ashen Pact/Esoterica|Gremory]] gave himself to magic and grew very good at it. There are four generations, each ten more than the last: ten in the first, forty in the fourth. The first generation hold the most power but are the most bound by their names; the first-generation Hunger, for one, is ruled by its need to feed. The fourth generation are the closest to sane, human thought, and the weakest.

Sedecium calls the gods of a world its [[Divinity/Higher Kind|higher kinds]], one to three to a planet, and says the higher kinds once gave the Thessians their names when they ascended. That service has ended, and a Thessian that rises now takes the name the universe gives it, whatever best fits its new nature. Either way, a Thessian's name is the theme or ideal it embodies, whether a creature, a concept or an emotion.

The Thessian body is smooth gray skin over three legs and three arms set radial around a torso, with no face. They do not walk so much as stand on the tripod they make, and they fly through a psychic force of their own rather than wings. In this form they read the world as a colourless topological map, seeing the fine ridges of a fingerprint from across a room, and they speak into whatever minds are near them rather than aloud. Their young are small; one a foot tall, found in the [[Geography/Althane/Realms/Ilkyne|City of Yellow]], cried into the minds of everyone around it.

A Thessian exists by holding a high local information density; one that loses enough of it loses coherence and temporarily ceases to exist, which is why killing one is nigh impossible. The only sure way Sedecium knows is a spike of [[Phenomena/Holy Gold|holy gold]], a rare gold metal whose growths appear perhaps once in three or four worlds, and the needles it knows of are found, not made. A group that takes up a needle, slays the god it is meant for, and stops is let be; what the powers that leave the needles about do not want is a group that keeps building on the power it has won. [[People/Althane/The Broker|T3C]] and his companions killed [[Divinity/Unknown Entities/Mephistopheles|Mephistopheles]] on [[Geography/Ushas/index|Ushas]] with such a needle, and Sedecium has since killed Monotony and Demon.

Sedecium has tracked them a long time. It knows [[Divinity/Ashen Pact/Pyre|Pyre]], [[Divinity/Ashen Pact/Herald|Herald]] and [[Divinity/Ashen Pact/Giant|Giant]] as Thessians under those names. It knows [[Divinity/Ashen Pact/Esoterica|Esoterica]], [[Divinity/Ashen Pact/War|War]] and [[Divinity/Ashen Pact/Regent|Regent]] as the fourth-generation Thessians Gremory, Astaroth and Paimon. Sedecium had not known the last three for Thessians, and that is the worst kind of news: they were not among the original set.

One of their number, the first-generation Thessian once called Leitmotif, has turned on the rest. It steals the life and portfolios of other Thessians and answers now to the name [[Divinity/Unknown Entities/Harrow|Harrow]]. Among those it has killed is [[Divinity/Unknown Entities/Tithe|Tithe]], a planet-sized Thessian it swallowed whole, and the corpse drew Sedecium to try to strip the divine essence out of the body before it was eaten.

A first-generation Thessian leaves a mark on a sphere it has visited: a magic number that appears there again and again. Ushas's is sixteen, the Sundom's three and Althane's twelve. Færrin has shown none, though that may be changing with the [[Divinity/Ashen Pact/index|Ashen Pact]] there. Five Thessians are known to have been on Ushas: [[Divinity/Unknown Entities/Harrow|Leitmotif]], [[Geography/Althane/The Archive|Archive]], [[Divinity/Unknown Entities/Dragon|Dragon]], [[Divinity/Unknown Entities/Mephistopheles|Mephistopheles]] and [[Divinity/Unknown Entities/Uriel|Uriel]].
